<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rim-Sin II</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rim-Sin II was a pretender who led a widespread rebellion of southern Mesopotamian cities against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> shortly after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>. According to the Babylonian Empire profile, the revolt spread across 26 southern cities and was crushed by Hammurabi's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a>. The victory came at great cost, as the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a> were pulled down and these cities ceased to function as administrative centres.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rim-sin-ii.jpg" alt="Rim-Sin II" width="320" height="278" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hammurabi%27s_Babylonia_1.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
